[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Moin. In this special video, I\'m going to show you how I transform gorgeous 3D printed vases into custom deco pieces wrapped in a slick real metal layer all using DIY electroplating. I\'ll walk you through the entire process step-by-step'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and show you how to combine different techniques to get the best results. To a lot of people, this looks like complete magic, but with a little time and patience, it\'s something you can easily do at home. So, let\'s skip the small'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talk and jump right into it. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I was in Tokyo for the Japan Rep Festival showing off my electroplated 3D prints once again. While I was there, I noticed that Saber Design was also'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exhibiting. Since I really enjoy printing their models, I decided to take two of their vases designs as examples for this project and put my own metal plated spin on them. Yeah, let\'s kick things off with the first model, the'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Roku vase, which I printed on the Snapmaker U1, a fantastic tool changer. Right off the bat, you\'ll notice I didn\'t print the silhouette of Japan as part of the main body. I want to electroplate that with different metals later, which would be pretty tricky if'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it were built into the vase itself. Take a look at the amazing fuzzy skin texture, too. For the inlays, you can print them separately using either FDM or resin. I\'m going with resin here, but keep in mind while resin is much easier'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to get smooth, making the prep work a lot less tedious than dealing with FDM layer lines, it can be tricky when it comes to fit tolerance later on, especially with parts this large.'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, printed, washed, and cured. Time for quick clearance test, and yep, perfect fit. Now, these parts have extremely fine layer lines, which we need to completely get rid of for'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electroforming. For that, I\'m using a 1,000 grit sandpaper. Once everything is nice and smooth and thoroughly degreased, it\'s time to apply the conductive paint. I\'m using conductive copper paint here, and by the way, I\'ve'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got a video coming up soon where I strip everything down to the essentials, including DIY paint recipes. Just saying. And this is what the final coating should look like.'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now onto the electroforming. The process itself is the usual drill, but here are a few key points to keep in mind. The electrolyte bath is the absolute heart of the whole process. Mine is a'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commercial setup, not homemade, and honestly, I strongly recommend going with a professional bath over DIY. A few vital parameters here, make sure to leave plenty of distance between your 3D print and the anode, at least 10 to 15'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cm for complex geometries. You also need heavy agitation in the tank. In my opinion, a good stirrer is the single most crucial component. Without it, you\'ll get nasty geometrical artifacts'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on your prints. And if your bath turns cloudy, don\'t panic, just let it settle and filter it later. I think a quick guide on how to treat with activated carbon. After about 4 hours, pull the part out, check on it. If it\'s nice and'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shiny, it\'s done. For the larger components, I had to upgrade to bigger tanks, but those turned out really well, too. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Just remember to filter the solution every now and then using a 1 micron PB mesh filter. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; For the For the absolute la For the absolute largest piece, I had to improvise and use an old UV cover from curing station. It\'s far from ideal, but it got the job done. Honestly,'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything about this setup looked completely wrong. The electrolyte had a nickel-like sheen, and there was way too little anode clearance, but the final result turned out surprisingly great. This is a perfect spot to bring in my'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'YouTube members. Using a small scriber, I\'m going to scratch all your names right into the back of the copper plate to 3D print. A huge thank you to everyone supporting the channel. I really appreciate you.'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, we\'re leaving the largest piece as is while the mini one is getting nickel plated and the smallest one is getting plated in gold. Before I forget to mention it in the video, PPE. Always wear your safety gear. That\'s not'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a polite suggestion, it\'s mandatory. Goggles, respirator, and gloves. You know the drill, right? Here\'s what the nickel plating process looks like. Pretty much the exact same steps as copper plating. Looks great, doesn\'t it?'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, time to fit the pieces into the main body of the vase. I tried to make this look cinematic and exciting, but well, it is what it is. You can press them right in and thanks to the extra layer of metal, they fit super'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'snug now. Perfect. We\'ll take a look at the final results at the end of the video. All right, onto the second model, the Hoji vase. This one features a modular center section and I\'m actually making two different versions of it. I printed'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this one with a smooth outer surface, but as you can clearly see here, smooth is definitely relative. All of this needs to be properly sanded down because I\'m planning to give it a full copper coat and that\'s what we\'re aiming for.'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'To keep the electrolyte solution from seeping into the layer lines, I\'m using a liquid sealer. I pour it all the way to the top, fill up the inside, and then dump the excess back up. Yep, that top piece gets the same treatment plus a'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thorough coat brush around the outside and then we let it cure for 23 hours. Sanding time. Today we\'re playing a fun game called minimize the microplastics. You want to knock down all those rough'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edges first. After that, we move on to about 500 layers of primer. And yes, sanding in between every single coat. No joking. The better your surface prep, the better your final plating'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'results will be. So, don\'t rush it. Next on goes another layer of conductive paint, then back into the bath, and boom, you got yourself some beautiful metal parts. As for that thick bottom'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'section, I put it into that weird yellow tank again, and it failed miserably. I don\'t even have video footage of it because I was so ridiculously frustrated. In the end, I managed to save it with a ton of polishing'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compound, so it turned out fine, but please don\'t ask. Once I assembled everything, the transformation was just wild. But, now that dark brown filament didn\'t match at all. So, I reprinted the center section with a racing checkered'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pattern, which is exactly what the you want tool changer was built for. A while back, I actually made some matching checkered rings, and they pair up with the vase amazingly well. I mean, who doesn\'t need a matching ring for the'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'vase, right? &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; All right. I got one more for you. I had one extra vase left over, so for this one, we\'re doing an electroplated center ring. I really wanted to experiment a'

[06:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bit here. I printed another center insert out of resin, gave it a nice thick copper layer, and then dipped it into the nickel bath. Originally, I tried to plate it with a continuous copper-to-nickel-to-gold'

[07:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gradient, but honestly, it looked like total trash. So, instead, I went with a sharp hard contrast between the nickel and gold. What do you think? Yay or nay? People often ask me, "I can\'t find these'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chemicals in my country." or "Why is my plating turned out dull and salmon pink?" Honestly, you just have to keep in mind that this is a hobby with a pretty steep learning curve, and my next video is going to focus entirely on the'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'absolute basics to help you get started. If you don\'t want to miss that, or if you just enjoyed this video, hit that follow button, or leave some cheeky comments down below. It really helps out the channel a lot.'

